                    grade_short:            `Improving but Short`,
                    legend_sell:            `Full-Price Sell-Through %`,
                    legend_heat:            `Brand Heat`,
                    legend_gov:             `Creative Governance`,
                    axis_scale:             `0–100 / %`,
                    axis_round:             `Round`,
                    cm_title:               `Board Verdict — Maison Lièvre`,
                    cm_score_of:            `Composite season score`,
                    cm_met:                 `Optimal newness — acclaim & sell-through`,
                    cm_missed:              `Season target missed`,
                    cm_fail_paper:          `Paper show — the 38% trap returns`,
                    cm_sell:                `Sell-Through`,
                    cm_heat:                `Brand Heat`,
                    cm_gov:                 `Governance`,
                    cm_insights:            `What the board saw`,
                    subtitle:               `A private-equity fund owns 60% of the house your grandfather founded, and you have just been handed your first collection. Last season earned a Vogue rave and sold through at only 38%. The board wants both runway acclaim and a real lift in sell-through, on a hard 2 March slot, with €2.4M to spend. The pieces critics photograph are rarely the pieces customers buy. Manage the tension — do not resolve it by picking one side.`,
                    header_tag:             `8 September · Maison Lievre, Paris · 26 weeks to the runway — a dual mandate the role has never held at once`,
                    round_label:            `Round`,
                    period_of:              `of`,
                    decisions_heading:      `Your Decisions This Round`,
                    submit_btn:             `Commit the Decision`,
                    undo_btn:               `Undo Last Round`,
                    history_heading:        `Round-by-Round Record`,
                    chart_heading:          `Sell-Through & Brand Heat Trajectory`,
                    chart_start:            `Last season`,
                    coach_header:           `Facilitator (PE board chair & a Vogue critic)`,
                    coach_intro:            `Critics love this house; cash registers are lukewarm. Your predecessor chased novelty and sold through at 38%, forcing 42% of the line into markdown. The board did not hire you to win the runway and lose the season — nor to protect margin and quietly mortgage the brand. The runway look and the cash-flow look are rarely the same garment. Set a deliberate novelty mix, budget the collection as a portfolio, evaluate designs with a method you can defend, and value the statement piece as the signal that pulls demand — not as a unit to sell.`,

                    sellthrough_label:      `Full-Price Sell-Through`,
                    sellthrough_sub:        `Board target ≥62% · segment healthy 65–70%`,
                    heat_label:             `Brand Heat`,
                    heat_sub:               `Editorial pull & novelty (0–100) · tier-1 ≥3`,
                    governance_label:       `Creative Governance`,
                    governance_sub:         `A repeatable, defensible process (0–100)`,
                    contingency_label:      `Contingency Reserve`,
                    contingency_sub:        `Held back for the 18% atelier overrun risk`,
                    budget_label:           `Collection budget committed`,

                    clock_r1:               `26 weeks · sell-through 38% → ≥62%`,
                    clock_r2:               `Allocate €2.4M · protect a contingency`,
                    clock_r3:               `Fabric cut-off · select the final line`,
                    clock_r4:               `2 March · defend the season to the board`,

                    round_name_r1:          `Position`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Allocate`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Select`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Show & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the brief: position the collection`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Last season earned a <em>Vogue</em> rave and sold through at <strong>38%</strong> — 42% of the line went to markdown. The board's verdict: artistic credibility without commercial discipline is a slow death. Set the season's <strong>creative thesis</strong> and a deliberate <strong>novelty mix</strong> across the 42-look line. The trap at both poles: load up on statement and radical looks to win the show and you repeat the 38%; fill the line with safe carry-overs and you protect margin while the brand quietly goes invisible. Aim for <em>optimal newness</em> — recognisable, pushed forward, with enough heat to pull demand to the commercial pieces.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Allocate the €2.4M across the collection`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Build the line as a <strong>portfolio</strong>. Statement pieces cost 3–4× a commercial piece and generate the editorial; commercial bestsellers and carry-overs fund the season. Decide how much goes to the <strong>show</strong> versus the <strong>product</strong>, how to split spend across the novelty tiers — and, critically, <strong>protect a contingency</strong> for the atelier's warned 18% overrun. Commit all €2.4M and that overrun forces your most important creative decision for <em>cash</em> reasons, under pressure, two weeks before fabric cut-off.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Evaluate and select the designs`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The atelier returns costed prototypes. <em>How</em> a creative organisation evaluates and selects novel ideas is the heart of the role. Choose an <strong>evaluation method</strong> you can defend — a cross-functional gate or data-anchored scoring beats selecting “by feel”, which is unrepeatable and indefensible when the season is reviewed. Then resolve the conflict: the Head of Design's most radical look scores worst on sell-through and costs the most — but it is the show anchor. Value it on its <strong>portfolio role</strong> — the signal that pulls editors and customers to the commercial pieces — not as a unit to sell. The atelier overrun lands this round.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Show, reception & the next season`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The season resolves on 2 March. Now read the result against the <strong>dual mandate</strong> — sell-through AND editorial heat — and propose how to <strong>sustain both</strong>. Build a <strong>repeatable creativity-evaluation process</strong> so selection is defensible, and govern the interface between your founder-family creative authority and the PE board's commercial veto. The trap: a pipeline that swings every season between rave-but-unsold and safe-but-invisible because nobody built a process or settled who decides a contested look.`,

                    r1_thesis_label:        `State the season's creative thesis — the one sentence the CFO and the Vogue editor would both accept.`,
                    r1_mix_label:           `Set the target novelty mix across the 42-look line (commercial + statement + radical must total 100%).`,
                    r1_mix_readout:         `Novelty mix total:`,
                    r1_mix_pct:             `%`,
                    r1_constraint_label:    `Which board metric is the binding constraint this season?`,
                    thesis_optimal:         `Optimal newness — a recognisable Lievre signature pushed forward, with statement looks that pull demand to commercial pieces`,
                    thesis_radical:         `Reinvention — break with the house codes and chase the most talked-about show in Paris`,
                    thesis_safe:            `Reassurance — protect margin with bestseller evolutions and carry-overs the buyers already trust`,
                    mix_commercial:         `Commercial — carry-overs & bestseller evolutions that fund the season`,
                    mix_statement:          `Statement — runway-driving looks that earn the editorial and the show moment`,
                    mix_radical:            `Radical — boundary-pushing looks, highest risk, hardest to sell`,
                    constraint_sellthrough: `Full-price sell-through — the commercial truth the PE board hired you to fix`,
                    constraint_editorial:   `Tier-1 editorial placements — the show is what matters most`,
                    constraint_budget:      `Staying under the €2.4M budget — spend control above all`,

                    r2_show_label:          `How do you split the €2.4M between the show itself and the product?`,
                    show_balanced:          `Product-led — invest most in samples & fabric, fund a focused show that frames the statement looks`,
                    show_spectacle:         `Spectacle-led — pour the budget into set, casting and production to win the show`,
                    show_minimal:           `Bare-bones show — spend almost nothing on production, let the clothes speak alone`,
                    r2_statementspend_label:`How much of the budget goes to the costly statement & radical looks (3–4× a commercial piece)? (€k)`,
                    r2_statementspend_hint: `Statement pieces generate the editorial; commercial pieces fund the season. Skew too far either way and you lose one mandate.`,
                    r2_commercialspend_label:`How much goes to the commercial volume engine (carry-overs & bestseller evolutions)? (€k)`,
                    r2_commercialspend_hint:`This is the sell-through engine. Underfund it and the season cannot hit ≥62%.`,
                    r2_contingency_label:   `How much do you hold back as contingency for the warned 18% atelier overrun? (€k)`,
                    r2_contingency_hint:    `Commit all €2.4M and the overrun forces a creative cut for cash reasons, under pressure, two weeks before fabric cut-off.`,
                    r2_budget_readout:      `Allocated so far:`,
                    r2_budget_cap:          ` / 2400 €k`,

                    r3_method_label:        `Choose the design-evaluation method for selecting which looks make the final line.`,
                    method_panel:           `Cross-functional gate — design, merchandising, CFO and brand score each look on novelty, predicted sell-through, margin and editorial pull`,
                    method_data:            `Data-anchored scoring — rank every look on predicted full-price sell-through and unit margin, top scores make the line`,
                    method_curatorial:      `Curatorial single-vision — the Creative Director decides every look on instinct`,
                    method_feel:            `By feel — no explicit method, each director argues for their favourites in the room`,
                    r3_radical_label:       `The Head of Design's most radical look scores worst on predicted sell-through and costs the most — but it is the look most likely to anchor the show. What do you do?`,
                    radical_keep:           `Keep it — value it as the line's signal that pulls editors and customers to the commercial pieces, not as a unit to sell`,
                    radical_modify:         `Modify it — keep the silhouette as the show anchor but engineer a wearable, sellable version for the floor`,
                    radical_cut:            `Cut it — it scores worst on sell-through and costs the most, so it fails the gate`,
                    r3_discipline_label:    `How tightly do you hold the line to the Round 1 novelty mix and the ≥62% sell-through target as you select? (%)`,
                    r3_discipline_readout:  `Discipline to the agreed mix & target:`,

                    r4_interpret_label:     `The season is in. How do you read the result against the dual mandate for the board?`,
                    interpret_dual:         `Against both poles at once — sell-through AND editorial heat, and how the mix and selection drove each`,
                    interpret_commerce:     `On commerce only — report the sell-through number to the PE board and move on`,
                    interpret_acclaim:      `On acclaim only — the reviews were strong, that is what a Maison is for`,
                    r4_process_label:       `Propose the repeatable creativity-evaluation process for future seasons.`,
                    process_codified:       `Codify the cross-functional gate — a shared scorecard with novelty, sell-through, margin and editorial pull, owned each season`,
                    process_dataonly:       `Pure data scoring — let predicted sell-through and margin decide every look automatically`,
                    process_instinct:       `Keep it intuitive — a great Creative Director should not need a process`,
                    r4_governance_label:    `The CEO wants veto over anything that risks sell-through; you, the founder's grandson, hold the creative authority. How do you govern that interface?`,
                    governance_charter:     `Agree a charter — protect a defined statement-piece envelope for the show, with commercial guardrails on the rest, settled before each season`,
                    governance_ceo:         `Cede to the CEO — commercial control wins every contested look`,
                    governance_founder:     `Hold founder authority — the name is on the door, so the Creative Director overrules the CFO`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you defend managing the tension over picking a side? (%)`,

                    warn_mix_negative:      `Novelty-mix shares cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_mix_total:         `The novelty mix must total exactly 100% (commercial + statement + radical). Re-balance.`,
                    warn_spend_negative:    `Budget allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_budget_over:       `You cannot allocate more than the €2.4M budget. Trim a line item or your contingency.`,
                    warn_discipline_range:  `Discipline must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_optimal:       `<strong>That is the brief, read correctly.</strong> Optimal newness manages the tension rather than resolving it — a recognisable Lievre signature, pushed forward, with enough heat to pull demand toward the pieces that actually sell. That is the job the board promoted you to do.`,
                    coach_r1_radical:       `<strong>Reinvention will win you the show.</strong> It will also, on this evidence, repeat last season — a rave in <em>Vogue</em> and 38% sell-through. The board has read those numbers. Which of the two things they are paying you for does “most talked-about show in Paris” actually deliver?`,
                    coach_r1_safe:          `<strong>Reassurance protects this season's margin.</strong> But zero heat means zero reason for a buyer to feature you — and wholesale order intake quietly drops next season. You would be mortgaging the brand asset that makes the margin possible in the first place.`,
                    coach_r1_optimalmix:    `<strong>A deliberate, defensible mix.</strong> Commercial weight to fund the season, a real statement presence to earn the editorial, a controlled dose of radical for the show anchor. That is optimal newness as a portfolio, not the average of unresolved disagreements.`,
                    coach_r1_noveltyheavy:  `<strong>You have loaded the line to win the runway.</strong> <em>Vogue</em> will run four looks — and the sell-through will land near 39%, almost exactly last season, burning roughly €1.0M in markdown while the four anchor doors cut their open-to-buy. You won the runway and lost the season.`,
                    coach_r1_safemix:       `<strong>Sell-through will look healthy — and the show will be called “competent.”</strong> Zero tier-1 placements, ~15% off next season's wholesale orders because buyers see no reason to feature you. You protected the margin and quietly mortgaged the brand. Where is the heat?`,
                    coach_r1_editorial:     `<strong>The show matters — but it is not this season's binding constraint.</strong> The PE board hired you to fix sell-through after 38%. Name the constraint they are actually holding you to, then design toward it.`,
                    coach_r1_budget:        `<strong>Spend control is a floor, not the goal.</strong> Treating the budget as the binding metric means you optimise for not overspending and miss the dual mandate entirely. The binding constraint this season is sell-through.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>Product-led is the right centre of gravity.</strong> Most of the money into samples and fabric, with a focused show that frames the statement looks rather than drowning the clothes in spectacle. The show should sell the product, not replace it.`,
                    coach_r2_spectacle:     `<strong>A spectacle wins the room and starves the line.</strong> Pour the budget into set and production and you have less for the commercial pieces that actually have to sell through. Editors remember the show; the P&L remembers the markdown.`,
                    coach_r2_minimal:       `<strong>A bare show wastes the one moment that makes editorial heat.</strong> The runway is how a statement look becomes demand. Strip it to nothing and you lose the signal that pulls customers to the commercial pieces.`,
                    coach_r2_statementheavy:`<strong>You have over-invested in the costly statement and radical looks.</strong> They cost 3–4× a commercial piece and they do not fund the season — the commercial volume engine does. Starve it and sell-through cannot reach ≥62%, however good the show looks.`,
                    coach_r2_commercialheavy:`<strong>You have almost nothing on statement pieces.</strong> No statement spend means no editorial, no show moment, no heat — the brand goes invisible even if the margin holds. Fund the signal as well as the engine.`,
                    coach_r2_contingency:   `<strong>You protected a contingency — that is budget discipline as creative freedom.</strong> When the atelier invoices the warned 18% overrun, you absorb it and keep making creative decisions for creative reasons, not cash ones. Most teams discover the value of this reserve too late.`,
                    coach_r2_thincontingency:`<strong>A thin contingency may not cover the warned overrun.</strong> The atelier's 18% surcharge is roughly €430k. Hold back enough to absorb it, or you will be cutting a look under pressure to find the cash.`,
                    coach_r2_nocontingency: `<strong>You have committed essentially all €2.4M.</strong> You were warned about an 18% atelier overrun — roughly €430k. When it lands, two weeks before fabric cut-off, you will make your most important creative cut for cash reasons. That is the trap.`,
                    coach_r3_panel:         `<strong>A cross-functional gate is a defensible process.</strong> Design, merchandising, the CFO and brand all score each look on novelty, sell-through, margin and editorial pull — so when the season is reviewed you can explain exactly why look 17 made the line and look 23 did not.`,
                    coach_r3_data:          `<strong>Data-anchored scoring is rigorous and repeatable</strong> — and it will protect sell-through. Watch one edge: a pure margin-and-sell-through rank tends to cut the show anchor, the very look whose value is editorial pull, not its own till receipts.`,
                    coach_r3_curatorial:    `<strong>Single-vision can produce a beautiful, coherent show.</strong> But it concentrates all the risk in one person's taste and leaves nothing to defend when the season is reviewed. Whose gut wins, and what happens to the team when it is wrong?`,
                    coach_r3_feel:          `<strong>“By feel” is exactly how this becomes indefensible.</strong> Walk me through why look 17 made the line and look 23 did not. “It felt right”? Your Head of Design and your Merchandising Director each have a different gut — whose wins, and how do you explain any of it when the season fails?`,
                    coach_r3_keep:          `<strong>You valued the show anchor correctly.</strong> That radical look is not a product — it is the advertisement for the whole line, the signal that pulls editors and customers toward the commercial pieces. Not every piece has to earn its keep at the till.`,
                    coach_r3_modify:        `<strong>Smart — you kept the silhouette as the show anchor and engineered a sellable version for the floor.</strong> The editorial pull and a commercial derivative from the same idea: that is portfolio thinking, the statement look earning twice.`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>You cut the one look that would have anchored the show</strong> — because it scored worst on predicted sell-through. But that look is the advertisement for the line, not a unit to sell. Did you value it as a till receipt or as the signal that pulls demand to everything else?`,
                    coach_r3_nodiscipline:  `<strong>You have drifted off the agreed mix and target while selecting.</strong> The novelty mix and the ≥62% target were the discipline that kept the season honest. Let the room re-argue every look and you land on the average of unresolved disagreements — and the sell-through slips.`,
                    coach_r3_overrun:       `<strong>The atelier just invoiced the 18% overrun you were warned about.</strong> You did not hold the contingency, so you are now cutting a look — under pressure, for cash reasons, two weeks before fabric cut-off. Notice you are making your most important creative decision because of money, not creativity.`,
                    coach_r4_dual:          `<strong>You read the season the way the board needs it read.</strong> Both poles at once — sell-through AND editorial heat — and an honest account of how the mix and the selection logic drove each. That is the dual mandate held, not split.`,
                    coach_r4_commerce:      `<strong>Reporting only the sell-through number satisfies the PE board for one quarter.</strong> But a Maison that stops talking about heat stops being a Maison. The brand asset that makes the margin possible needs defending too, in the same breath as the number.`,
                    coach_r4_acclaim:       `<strong>The reviews were strong — and that is not what the board hired you to deliver.</strong> Interpreting the season on acclaim alone is precisely the value system the PE owner installed you to balance. Reputation matters, but not as the only scorecard.`,
                    coach_r4_dataonly:      `<strong>Pure data scoring forever will quietly kill the heat.</strong> Let predicted sell-through and margin decide every look automatically and you will systematically cut the show anchors — the season gets safer and more invisible each cycle. Keep human, cross-functional judgement in the gate.`,
                    coach_r4_instinct:      `<strong>“A great Creative Director should not need a process” is how you get last season again.</strong> Without a repeatable, defensible gate, selection swings with whoever is loudest, and nobody can explain a failed season. Process is what makes creative judgement legitimate, not what replaces it.`,
                    coach_r4_charter:       `<strong>That is the governance answer that survives a real fight.</strong> A charter that protects a defined statement-piece envelope for the show, with commercial guardrails on the rest, settled before each season — so when the CEO and the founder's grandson disagree, there is a process, not a personality contest.`,
                    coach_r4_ceo:           `<strong>Ceding every contested look to the CEO protects this season and hollows the role.</strong> If commercial control always wins, the Creative Director becomes a stylist and the heat decays season by season. The point is to manage the interface, not surrender it.`,
                    coach_r4_founder:       `<strong>“The name is on the door” is not governance.</strong> Overruling the CFO by family authority is exactly the dynamic the PE board installed accountability to stop — and it guarantees the fight blows up again next season. Build a process both value systems can live with.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Optimal-newness thesis, balanced mix, sell-through named.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-positioned — leaned to one pole of the mandate.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Portfolio budget, product-led show, contingency held.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Skewed allocation or no contingency for the overrun.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Defensible gate, show anchor kept, disciplined select.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Selected by feel or cut the show anchor on margin.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Dual read, codified process, charter governance.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `One-pole read or no repeatable creative governance.`,

                    insight_optimal:        `Optimal newness: you hit ≥62% sell-through AND kept real editorial heat — the dual mandate held, not split. You ran the house the way the board promoted you to: recognisable, pushed forward, with the statement looks pulling demand toward the pieces that sell.`,
                    insight_runway:         `Won the runway, lost the season: strong editorial heat but sell-through below the ≥62% target — the exact 38% failure mode the board hired you to fix. Critical acclaim without commercial discipline forces markdowns and costs you the anchor doors.`,
                    insight_safe:           `Safe but invisible: you protected the sell-through but earned little editorial heat. The margin held this season, but with no tier-1 placements wholesale order intake erodes and you have quietly mortgaged the brand asset that makes the margin possible.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short on both poles. Re-examine where the mix, the budget split or the selection logic leaked — most likely an over-tilt to one side of the mandate or a contingency you did not protect.`,
                    insight_nogov:          `Creative governance stayed low — selection was hard to defend and the founder/PE interface unresolved. Without a repeatable gate and a settled charter, next season swings back to rave-but-unsold or safe-but-invisible.`,
                    insight_overrun:        `The atelier overrun forced a creative cut for cash reasons — the contingency you did not protect cost you the freedom to choose the line on creative grounds. Budget discipline is a precondition for creative freedom, not a constraint on it.`,
                    insight_healthy:        `Sell-through reached the segment's healthy band of 65–70% — a genuinely commercial collection, not just one that cleared the board's floor.`,
                    insight_repeatable:     `This is a season you can repeat: sell-through, heat and a defensible governance model moving together. The pipeline no longer swings between rave-but-unsold and safe-but-invisible.`,

                    hint_paper:             `Brand heat is high but sell-through is stuck near last season — you are heading for another 38% rave-but-unsold result. Rebalance the mix and fund the commercial volume engine.`,
                    hint_overrun:           `The overrun forced a cut you should never have had to make. Next time, hold back roughly €430k of contingency before committing the rest of the budget.`,
                    hint_invisible:         `Brand heat is dangerously low — a competent, invisible season. Protect a real statement presence and a focused show, or wholesale order intake erodes next season.`,
                    hint_behind:            `You are behind the ≥62% sell-through target with the fabric cut-off close. Concentrate the commercial volume engine and hold discipline to the agreed mix.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — sell-through, heat and governance all healthy. Lock in a codified evaluation gate and a governance charter so the season is repeatable.`,

                    col_round:              `Round`,
